  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure will be directly overhead tonight. With clear\nskies and light winds, good radiational cooling conditions will\nbe present. This will result in cool temperatures (lows in the\nlower 40s to near 50; upper 30s in northern Oneida County), and\nsome valley fog.\n\nThe high pressure system will depart the region on Saturday as a\nwarm front approaches the area. This will result in increasing\nclouds, and eventually rain showers moving into the area. The\nmajority of the area will likely be rain-free during the daytime\non Saturday, but a few showers may sneak in west of I-81 during\nthe afternoon.\n\nThe most concentrated period of rain showers and some embedded\nthunderstorms will be Saturday night/early Sunday morning. PWATs\nwill be around 1.7 inches and coupled with a decent LLJ, locally\nheavy rainfall will be possible. The greatest threat for this\nwill be across Northeast PA-Catskills. While widespread flash\nflooding is not anticipated, cannot rule out some localized\nflooding issues late Saturday night.\n\nThere will likely be a bit of a lull in the rainfall Sunday\nmorning, before a cold front will bring another round of\nscattered showers and thunderstorms in the afternoon. Depending\non how much clearing occurs, some of these thunderstorms may\nbecome strong to potentially severe.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFollowing the passage of the cold front, cooler air will move\ninto the region, which will bring highs in the 60s to lower 70s\nboth Monday and Tuesday. High pressure will keep conditions dry\nduring this period. Another system may bring some scattered\nshowers and potentially some thunderstorms by the middle of\nnext week, along with warmer temperatures.",
